---
import SectionHeading from '../SectionHeading.astro';
import SubHeading from '../logo/SubHeading.astro';
import ColorCards from '../color/ColorCards';
import ColorBalance from '../color/ColorBalance.astro';
import Pairings from '../color/Pairings.astro';
import { BRAND_COLORS } from '../../../brand/palette';
---

<section id="color" class="scroll-mt-28 py-16">
  <SectionHeading
    eyebrow="Color"
    title="Color"
    lead={`These ${BRAND_COLORS.length} colors are the MassChallenge palette, from the brand guide by MassChallenge Marketing. Click any value to copy it.`}
  />

  <ColorCards client:visible />

  <p
    class="m-0 mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-[length:var(--type-callout-size)] leading-[var(--type-callout-line)] text-[var(--label-secondary)]"
  >
    Contrast is shown with White and with Black as a ratio, then graded: AA means any text, AA
    Large means text 18pt or larger and graphics only, Fail means neither. The brand guide sets the
    palette, and Propellant applies it to websites, Office, print, the studio and the Claude skill.
  </p>
  <p
    class="m-0 mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-[length:var(--type-callout-size)] leading-[var(--type-callout-line)] text-[var(--label-secondary)]"
  >
    Status is separate. The signed in product keeps its own interface colors, and its error,
    warning and success colors only ever signal status. Raspberry, Orange and Lime are decorative
    brand accents and never signal status.
  </p>

  <SubHeading
    id="color-balance"
    title="How to balance it"
    lead="There is no fixed share of each color. Navy, Black and the blues carry most of every layout, and one warm accent adds a small dose of warmth."
  />
  <ColorBalance />

  <SubHeading
    id="color-pairings"
    title="Pairings that work"
    lead="The approved pairings from the brand guide, with their contrast ratios. On light backgrounds colored text is always Teal, and Cyan only ever sits on Navy, Black or Deep Teal."
  />
  <Pairings />
</section>
